      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>&#8250;</span> <a href="/european-tech-alternatives.html">European Alternatives</a><span>&#8250;</span> Email Marketing</p>
      <h1>European Email Marketing Platforms</h1>
      <p class="hero__sub">Your mailing list is a genuine pile of personal data, the products are close substitutes, and the switch takes an afternoon. This is the best effort-to-benefit ratio in the whole sovereignty conversation.</p>
      <p class="disclosure">We have no affiliate relationship with any platform on this page. Nothing here is a paid placement. <a href="/methodology.html">How we test →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-body">
    <div class="container container--narrow">

      <div class="answer-box">
        <p><strong>Brevo</strong> (Paris) is the closest all-round Mailchimp replacement — automation, CRM and transactional email, EU-hosted. <strong>MailerLite</strong> (Vilnius) is the value pick and the simplest to use. <strong>CleverReach</strong> and <strong>rapidmail</strong> (both German) hold data exclusively in Germany, which matters for regulated buyers. <strong>GetResponse</strong> (Poland) has the deepest feature set including webinars.</p>
        <p>All five are EU-headquartered, so there is no international transfer to document at all.</p>
        <p><strong>One thing that trips people up:</strong> switching platform does <em>not</em> require re-permissioning your list. Consent was given to you, not to Mailchimp. You do have to carry the proof across.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="toc">
        <strong>On this page</strong>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#why">Why this is the switch to make first</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison">The platforms compared</a></li>
          <li><a href="#picks">The platforms in detail</a></li>
          <li><a href="#consent">The consent rules that actually apply</a></li>
          <li><a href="#migrate">Migrating without wrecking deliverability</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mailchimp">Do you actually have to leave Mailchimp?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <h2 id="why">Why this is the switch to make first</h2>
      <p>Three reasons it beats every other category on effort-to-benefit.</p>
      <p><strong>The data is genuinely personal.</strong> Names, email addresses, open and click history, often purchase behaviour and tags describing interests. That is a richer profile than your analytics holds, and it is directly identifiable.</p>
      <p><strong>The products are close substitutes.</strong> Unlike cloud infrastructure, there is no deep technical lock-in. A list is a CSV. Templates are HTML. The genuine work is rebuilding automations, and most businesses have fewer of those than they think.</p>
      <p><strong>The European options are not compromises.</strong> Brevo and GetResponse are full-featured platforms competing on merit; MailerLite is cheaper than Mailchimp for most list sizes. You are not paying a sovereignty tax here.</p>
      <div class="callout callout--info">
        <strong>Contrast with infrastructure.</strong> Moving off AWS is months of engineering for a risk most small businesses will never realise. Moving off Mailchimp is an afternoon plus a warm-up period, for the same category of legal benefit. Start where the ratio is good — the priority order is in our <a href="/european-tech-alternatives.html">European alternatives overview</a>.
      </div>

      <h2 id="comparison">The platforms compared</h2>
      <div class="comparison-wrap">
        <table class="comparison-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Platform</th><th>HQ</th><th>Data held in</th><th>Free tier</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="pick">
              <td class="tool-name">Brevo</td>
              <td>France</td>
              <td class="check">EU</td>
